/**
 * @page py_hpc HPC Acceleration (GPU, Multi-GPU & MPI Clusters)
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section py_gpu Single-GPU Backend (NVIDIA cuStateVec & Custom Kernels)
 *
 * Maestro supports hardware-accelerated simulation on a single NVIDIA GPU through cuStateVec and
 * custom CUDA kernels.
 *
 * @subsection py_gpu_avail Availability & Initialization
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * import maestro
 *
 * # Check availability
 * if maestro.is_gpu_available():
 *     print("GPU acceleration available")
 *     maestro.init_gpu()
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection py_gpu_usage Single-GPU Simulation
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * # GPU Statevector
 * gpu_sv_config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulator_type=maestro.SimulatorType.Gpu,
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.Statevector,
 * )
 * result = maestro.simple_execute(qasm, config=gpu_sv_config, shots=1000)
 *
 * # GPU Matrix Product State (MPS)
 * gpu_mps_config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulator_type=maestro.SimulatorType.Gpu,
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.MatrixProductState,
 *     max_bond_dimension=64,
 * )
 * result_mps = maestro.simple_execute(qasm, config=gpu_mps_config, shots=1000)
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_dist_gpu Distributed GPU Simulation (Multi-GPU & MPI Clusters)
 *
 * For large statevector simulations exceeding single-GPU memory limits, Maestro supports two
 * dynamically loaded distributed plugins from `maestro-gpu-distributed` (Linux only):
 *
 * | Python Simulator Type | C++ Simulator Type | Plugin Library | Description |
 * |-----------------------|--------------------|----------------|-------------|
 * | `SimulatorType.DistributedGpu` | `kDistGpuSim` | `libmaestro_gpu_distributed.so` | State distributed across local GPUs in a single node |
 * | `SimulatorType.DistributedMpiGpu` | `kDistMpiGpuSim` | `libmaestro_gpu_distributed_mpi.so` | State distributed across MPI ranks and GPUs (single or multi-node) |
 *
 * Both plugins support **Statevector simulation only** (`SimulationType.Statevector`). Other simulation
 * methods will raise an exception.
 *
 * @subsection py_dist_gpu_prereq Prerequisites & Plugin Loading
 *
 * - Put the plugin `.so` files and their dependencies on the runtime library search path (`LD_LIBRARY_PATH`),
 *   or set `MAESTRO_DIST_GPU_LIBRARY` and `MAESTRO_DIST_MPI_GPU_LIBRARY` to their absolute paths.
 * - For MPI execution, a CUDA-aware MPI implementation (such as OpenMPI or MPICH with CUDA support) is required.
 * - The number of MPI ranks must be a power of two, at most 32, leaving at least one qubit local.
 * - Licensed builds require `MAESTRO_LICENSE_KEY` or the plugin's cached activation.
 *
 * @subsection py_dist_gpu_local Local Multi-GPU Execution
 *
 * Probe availability with `is_distributed_gpu_available()`, which checks plugin and device presence without allocating state:
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * import maestro
 *
 * if maestro.is_distributed_gpu_available():
 *     config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *         simulator_type=maestro.SimulatorType.DistributedGpu,
 *         simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.Statevector,
 *         distributed_options={
 *             "distributed_devices": "0,1",  # Comma-separated device ordinals
 *         },
 *     )
 *     result = maestro.simple_execute(qasm, shots=1000, config=config)
 *     print(result["counts"])
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection py_dist_gpu_mpi Distributed MPI GPU Execution
 *
 * MPI execution follows the SPMD (Single Program, Multiple Data) pattern: the application runs
 * **the identical Maestro script on every rank** via `mpirun`. All simulator invocations must occur
 * in matching order with identical arguments across ranks. Ordinary outputs are replicated on every rank;
 * output and display logic should be guarded by rank zero.
 *
 * Run with an MPI launcher:
 * @code{.sh}
 * mpirun -n 2 python mpi_sim.py
 * @endcode
 *
 * In `mpi_sim.py`:
 * @code{.py}
 * import mpi4py
 * mpi4py.rc.thread_level = "serialized"
 * from mpi4py import MPI
 * import maestro
 *
 * config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulator_type=maestro.SimulatorType.DistributedMpiGpu,
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.Statevector,
 * )
 *
 * try:
 *     result = maestro.simple_execute(qasm, shots=1000, config=config)
 *     if MPI.COMM_WORLD.rank == 0:
 *         print("Counts:", result["counts"])
 * finally:
 *     # Terminal backend cleanup must run after state destruction and before MPI finalization
 *     maestro.finalize_distributed_mpi_gpu()
 * @endcode
 *
 * @note Set `mpi4py.rc.thread_level = "serialized"` prior to importing `MPI` to ensure thread-level compatibility with CUDA-aware MPI transports.
 *
 * @subsection py_dist_gpu_options Configuration Options (`distributed_options`)
 *
 * Pass optional overrides as string key-value pairs in `SimulatorConfig.distributed_options`:
 *
 * | Key | Description | Example Values |
 * |-----|-------------|----------------|
 * | `distributed_devices` | Comma-separated GPU ordinals. For MPI, entries are relative to the owning rank | `"0,1"`, `"0"` |
 * | `distributed_global_qubits` | Ordered comma-separated global logical qubits | `"0"`, `"0,1"` |
 * | `distributed_backend` | Distribution engine | `"ex"` (default), `"conventional"` |
 * | `distributed_flags` | Bitmask: 1 permits local shared device testing, 2 fixed layout, 4 fullmesh topology, 8 pinned layout | `"0"` (default), `"8"` |
 * | `distributed_max_queued_gates` | Gate queue limit for Ex engine (1–65536) | `"2048"` |
 * | `distributed_transfer_workspace_bytes` | Transfer workspace in bytes (1 MiB–1 GiB) | `"67108864"` |
 * | `distributed_snapshot_storage` | Snapshot storage location | `"gpu"` (default), `"host"` |
 * | `mpi_communicator` | Fortran communicator handle from mpi4py `Comm.py2f()` | `str(MPI.COMM_WORLD.py2f())` |
 * | `mpi_p2p_bits` | Number of low rank bits identifying intra-host peers | `"0"` (default) |
 * | `precision` | Precision override | `"single"` (default), `"double"` |
 * | `seed` | Explicit 64-bit random seed (omitted seeds are generated collectively) | `"42"` |
 *
 * For full architecture details, consult the dedicated guide at `docs/distributed_gpu.md`.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_quest QuEST Backend (External Library Wrapper)
 *
 * Maestro integrates the QuEST engine for distributed statevector simulation across cluster nodes.
 *
 * @subsection py_quest_usage Running with QuEST
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * import maestro
 *
 * if maestro.is_quest_available():
 *     maestro.init_quest()
 *
 * quest_config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulator_type=maestro.SimulatorType.QuestSim,
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.Statevector,
 * )
 * result = maestro.simple_execute(qasm, config=quest_config, shots=1024)
 * @endcode
 *
 * @note QuEST only supports `SimulationType.Statevector`. Attempting to use other simulation methods will raise an exception.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_gpu_fallback Graceful Hardware Fallback Pattern
 *
 * For portable workflows across GPU workstations, clusters, and developer laptops:
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * import maestro
 *
 * if maestro.is_distributed_gpu_available():
 *     backend = maestro.SimulatorType.DistributedGpu
 * elif maestro.is_gpu_available() and maestro.init_gpu():
 *     backend = maestro.SimulatorType.Gpu
 * elif maestro.is_quest_available() and maestro.init_quest():
 *     backend = maestro.SimulatorType.QuestSim
 * else:
 *     backend = maestro.SimulatorType.QCSim
 *
 * config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(simulator_type=backend)
 * result = maestro.simple_execute(qasm, config=config, shots=1024)
 * @endcode
 */
